#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea1cdb0e-2b3e-51b7-ada3-20934840b929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303685} {:answer/id #uuid "08e3b05f-c5e3-5157-bb76-52a66434c8a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303686} {:answer/id #uuid "8347eb97-b283-5ee4-9892-9892db531b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303687} {:answer/id #uuid "f76d78ea-5b74-52f5-8d91-66350064c866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303688} {:answer/id #uuid "50310b38-7a08-55bc-9721-4729c934c99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e3b05f-c5e3-5157-bb76-52a66434c8a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303686} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6c4aa4-a199-5932-a6e5-3ebdf887d971" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d55ca840-bf7c-5f00-aa1e-3953e088e6a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104813" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/931802565a13dac284496f41ebb19238402056689a96288b2fbb1dd9f018ae12.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(-1)=0$ and $g(-1)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,2)$ equals $\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f92ca9e4-bd3b-5fdb-93e6-2b2cbd38e187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303692} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9a0ee1-7f2c-5c94-b546-d4f0cf99e6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303693} {:answer/id #uuid "e91ad000-9ac3-555a-93cc-1cc4c8e5b952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303694} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2236d3-d2c6-5cd2-8740-166622627c98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303695} {:answer/id #uuid "7598c2f7-07f6-5518-85a7-2b621e8fa58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7598c2f7-07f6-5518-85a7-2b621e8fa58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303696} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c2ec269-4e2c-53e0-baf3-eab83225eae9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7282253e-1bb5-55a8-8d37-5f1d7c2b6670" :question/math-academy-id "q-105502" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/7b44dcfdfef91ada119ea7346b59f337a82bc8cca1e364fd6ea29c9eead5a2fd.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-3)=-2$ and $g(-3)=0.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,-2)$ equals $3.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-3)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & ={f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)+f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d595279a-331d-5f62-821b-ff8126c1cfcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303699} {:answer/id #uuid "93395dde-9fcd-5ba7-ad9a-68a86157ffb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303700} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba5c082-36a3-5a25-92a7-b1855ee4c9d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303701} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc68643-4c6c-53cf-8dc9-4319c1dd0168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303702} {:answer/id #uuid "33cb0912-96df-525d-878e-a973a1dcc143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33cb0912-96df-525d-878e-a973a1dcc143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303703} :answer-field/id #uuid "91a6e6ac-b53d-57c1-8dca-cb521b4240b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4862b6d5-1eb7-573f-b085-daa585633ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-15659" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/cf3cc180e5489aa83c17b47db641aae68d03b095085102a29a2942a783369c07.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)+{g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2.5,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/19a32fa6a7b2b6481052ab3539838bb00d486f92ea3f08b54efa2a1cfc69d221.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-2}{4-2}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2.5,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-1}{4-2.5}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)+{g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eef874d-155f-5e06-b4c9-f3d9b856bf53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303706} {:answer/id #uuid "4af4b92f-d0d4-52a3-850c-35e87b66735b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303707} {:answer/id #uuid "03754b5e-0cee-54e5-ad05-b56ef36bbc6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303708} {:answer/id #uuid "80a94deb-5abf-54d4-9fc3-ee9200625078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303709} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0b84a1-3c2f-5373-85aa-403aabf56cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80a94deb-5abf-54d4-9fc3-ee9200625078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303709} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8a970e-9196-5bfe-871a-28900d6b1880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf012889-ec8e-5d4a-9556-68a3d9dfb3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-18603" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/c4585c2a9cf0a66da02f96a17dad5986ed8c401a844276f08b367eed765c111d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/299b996bf08e4b4e8560d013829ddd1f42751767cd3f00dcd04c23c95f2c62b4.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-1}{3-(-2)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-2)}{3-(-2)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f1be80b-4c90-5772-9e15-1f1ccf4de6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f1be80b-4c90-5772-9e15-1f1ccf4de6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303713} :answer-field/id #uuid "36301a07-85a2-5c01-8655-8a1bc98ad086" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1efeb669-9cac-5a9b-9db2-a968a553ba2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-344896" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/847d1ae3ff7602ae8793ec356dd3034650d8ba85adcb3be579097e46728667a0.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(3)$ where $p(x)=f(x)g(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $g(3)=1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $2.$ So, ${g}^{′}(3)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
